1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,846
Microsoft's agent library for Python

2
00:00:02,646 --> 00:00:05,166
shipped a credential stealer that

3
00:00:04,966 --> 00:00:06,480
ran even if you never

4
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:07,253
imported it.

5
00:00:07,953 --> 00:00:09,761
Two bad versions sat on

6
00:00:09,561 --> 00:00:11,284
PyPI for under an hour.

7
00:00:11,324 --> 00:00:13,360
If an unpinned install grabbed

8
00:00:13,160 --> 00:00:15,549
one, treat every reachable secret

9
00:00:15,349 --> 00:00:16,114
as stolen.

10
00:00:16,664 --> 00:00:18,369
Each one dropped a startup

11
00:00:18,169 --> 00:00:19,938
file that Python runs at boot.

12
00:00:19,978 --> 00:00:22,040
Tests, linters or notebooks all

13
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:23,066
set off the stealer.

14
00:00:23,616 --> 00:00:25,578
The first release landed at

15
00:00:25,378 --> 00:00:27,033
twenty thirty-five UTC.

16
00:00:27,073 --> 00:00:29,064
Scanners flagged it in minutes,

17
00:00:28,864 --> 00:00:30,485
and both versions were gone

18
00:00:30,285 --> 00:00:31,844
by about twenty-one thirty.

19
00:00:32,394 --> 00:00:35,231
It swept secret environment variables,

20
00:00:35,031 --> 00:00:37,135
dozens of credential files,

21
00:00:36,935 --> 00:00:38,966
and cloud and pipeline tokens

22
00:00:38,766 --> 00:00:40,798
from Azure and GitHub Actions.

23
00:00:41,348 --> 00:00:43,752
Wiz counted seventeen requests with

24
00:00:43,552 --> 00:00:44,748
real victim data.

25
00:00:44,788 --> 00:00:46,926
That covers only one address,

26
00:00:46,726 --> 00:00:48,576
and stolen cloud tokens can

27
00:00:48,376 --> 00:00:49,724
stay valid for up to

28
00:00:49,524 --> 00:00:50,083
a day.

29
00:00:50,633 --> 00:00:52,312
Every Python launch in an

30
00:00:52,112 --> 00:00:54,989
infected environment runs the stealer again.

31
00:00:55,029 --> 00:00:56,867
So check from the outside,

32
00:00:56,667 --> 00:00:58,779
with lockfiles and file searches.

33
00:00:59,329 --> 00:01:01,296
Search lockfiles and CI logs

34
00:01:01,096 --> 00:01:02,547
for the bad versions.

35
00:01:02,587 --> 00:01:04,431
Rebuild from a hashed pin

36
00:01:04,231 --> 00:01:05,627
of one point two six

37
00:01:05,427 --> 00:01:07,644
point zero, then rotate every

38
00:01:07,444 --> 00:01:08,690
reachable token.

39
00:01:09,240 --> 00:01:11,151
Pinning with hashes and waiting

40
00:01:10,951 --> 00:01:12,672
a day before fresh releases

41
00:01:12,472 --> 00:01:14,384
would have stopped this one cold.

42
00:01:14,424 --> 00:01:16,018
The full story lists every

43
00:01:15,818 --> 00:01:16,652
file and key.

